ing yeah  busing didn't you know it might have  worked for some things it it some people  just had the agenda of black kids going  to South Boston high but I don't think  people really knew that you know how bad  at school South Boston High was at the  time only 10% of the people from South  Boston high were going on to college I  mean so rather than developing a plan  where we're both black inner-city kids  and white and a city poor kids would  gain access to to better school systems  perhaps in the suburbs you know of  course they took the poorest white  neighborhood poorest black neighborhood  and set them on each other and of course  it failed and I sometimes wonder if  these things aren't designed to fail  because that that was either incredibly  dumb or incredibly brilliant  setting up those two neighborhoods where  do you live now I live in South Boston  Southie yep why did you go batty I moved  back in 1994 because I was drawn back to  the neighborhood I've always felt a draw  because of my memories of my brothers  and my families when my family left  South Boston in 1991 and went to  Colorado I felt uprooted I left the  neighborhood moved to another  neighborhood always missed Southie would  wander through South East - kind of  just kind of find my family spirit on my  brothers spirits and I felt like it was  there and I was I was drawn to move back  there after the I gave a tour to a  reporter from US News and World Report  who was writing this story about what he  called the white underclass because  South Boston was being called the white  underclass capital of America  and when I gave him that tour I realized  how much I missed the neighborhood  despite that I was pointing out spots in  the neighborhood where some of the worst  atrocities happened I just kind of  abruptly told him you know I really  missed this place and I've always had  that love/hate thing with the  neighborhood and was just had to go back  there and when I went back there I found  out why I went back there I went back  there to do some community organizing  and to write this book and I didn't  realize that until now picture there of  the most where the most kids are located  what's that picture oh that's a picture  of a bunch of kids from the project  visiting my brother John who is the the  guy in the and that with the afro and  the plaid pants he was up at Tufts  University he was the one on the family  making it out and we would all come up  and invade his new world and of course  his fellow students would find out where  he really comes from where's John Wood  today he's in South America what's he do  he he's built he's retired from the Navy  SEALs but he's working on building a  development he does labor labor work you  know what's the story of Stevie my  little brother Stevie in 1990 was  falsely accused of murder and what were  the circumstances his best friend was  who was 13 years old died from a gunshot  wound to the head and Stephen found him  dying and I got very involved in his  case once the police once I started to  realize what the police were doing once  I found out that the police had  falsified his call to 9-1-1  and so forth he found his friend ina and  called 9-1-1 where was his friend his  friend was in the house he had he had  fallen under a bamboo chair large bamboo  swivel chair this was one of the hardest  tragedies for me to live through really  it was it was definitely the worst  because it was something happening to a  younger brother and because of the the  the horrible tragedy of this 13 year old  boy's death  in that apartment and and what year was  this 90 yeah and he was your brother  though was convicted yeah he was  convicted and and when I really saw the  level of police misconduct and police  abuse in this case falsification of  evidence and so forth and when he called  9-1-1  well they submitted a transcript of his  called tonight one one and it had him  yeah the police submitted this as  evidence that there was good there was  you know gonna go into court it had my  little brother's saying twice on this  sheet of paper which I have copies up to  this day I just shot my friend in the  head so I had my little brother calling  in and confessing to have shot his  friend when I first saw that I thought  maybe he did do it and maybe he blacked  it out or something and we ordered the  cassettes of his call tonight one one it  took about a month to get them but when  we heard that cassette there wasn't even  anything you could mistake for I just  shot my friend in the head so it was him  given his address screaming crying and  so forth but these the four detectives  at the center of that case were very  involved in a very high-profile racist  investigation in the city what's what we  call the Stewart case when a man called  Charles Stewart a white man from the  suburbs murdered his pregnant wife and  on the way from birthing classes and  when the police arrived Charles Stuart's  told the police that a black men had  carjacked them from the nearby black  housing project  and he'll and as a result the police and  the mayor completely tore that community  part people were brought dragged out of  the houses kids were stopped and  searched it was it became like a police  state in Mission Hill and witnesses were  coerced into giving false statements  about a local junkie named Willie  Bennett anyways it turned out that child  stewart had in fact shot his wife and  that the police were so quick to believe  this racial accusation anyway the cops  at the center of railroading this guy in  Mission Hill project well the same cops  involved in my little brother's case and  that's what got me to cross town to  cross neighborhood and cross race and  get involved with activists across town  on some of these issues whether they  were police community relations issues  to work on as an activist or the stuff  around gun violence how did you get the  conviction overturned it took us four  years to do that but my little brother  had been released from dys it's dys  other Department of Youth Services in  Massachusetts and they have they they  make the decision about how long a  person is to stay with them and they  when he was released his psychiatrist  there had to apologize to him for the  whole entire criminal justice system and  this is before his conviction was  overturned because the psychiatrist was  saying even you know even if I took you  and even if I believed that you were  guilty and that he shot your friend I  still don't know what you're doing here  and he said you know so many times you  see it on the news where two children  are playing with the gun and one shoots  the other and you see that there's no  prosecution that takes place lots of  times the victim's family is you know it  doesn't want to prosecution the same  year I saw that happen on the news and  in this case Stephen wasn't even guilty  I believe wholeheartedly but this  psychiatrist was saying that you know  even if I believe you're guilty I still  don't know why you you would drag  through this system as you were as a  child and he said you know  I have to believe that it has everything  to do with who you are and where you  come from who overturned the case it was  overturned by the state Supreme Court  and I was in a unanimous decision by  three judges based on the lawyers in the  the original trial lawyers incompetence  in relation to some of the obvious  police abuse and misconduct and  falsification of evidence where's Stevie  today he's in Colorado he's in college  he's along with my other brother Shamus  doing very well  they've good grade point averages and  they want to be lawyers so again I think  that's that's a theme that comes out a  lot in the book whether it's my mother  who's you know helping out some of the  mothers in the neighborhood who were  losing their children you know sharing  some of her strengths or myself getting  involved as an activist or my little  brothers wanted to be a lawyer I think a  common theme is this this survival  through action rather than medication or  therapy or whatever I think that's a big  issue that comes to around the book why  did you get in trouble all through these  years I didn't get in trouble because I  think at it because at a young age this  is something that I figured out while I  was writing the book at a young age I I  was able to kind of step outside of the  chaos with one foot even while keeping  another foot there I'm still from that  neighborhood I'm still from my family  but I was always able to be kind of to  see that world from from the outside and  to see the bigger picture so that I  wasn't trapped in it I never felt  trapped in it I never felt like my life  is gonna be determined by this that's a  common factor that you see in a lot of  what what teachers and and and  psychologists are calling resilience and  young people that's a common factor that  ability to step outside of any kind of  situation chaotic situation growing up  and to say that's not necessarily me  that's not going to determine Who I am  what's your long term goal um to keep  writing I love writing and I and I  really miss it during all this publicity  but I can't can't wait to get back to  writing I'd like to write more from  stories  from my life to maybe get deeper into  some pieces of the book and I'd also  like to write about other places where  that need this kind of truth-telling and  my only goals in general to tell the  truth and to help kids live I just want  kids to live that's that's really why  why I wrote the book is so this is the  cover of the book all souls is the title  and our guest has been Michael Patrick  McDonald the story of a family from  Southie thank you very much thanks a lot  Michael Patrick McDonald helped launch  Boston's gun buyback program and is the  founder of the South Boston vigil group  All Souls a family story from Southie  was recently released in paperback by  Ballantine Books and division of Random  House
 

